= September 2024 — Ninety minutes on the record

The caucus meets to argue about what the party is getting from the coalition.
The stream that should have been cut after the opening statement is not cut.
For roughly ninety minutes, anyone with the link hears deputies name which
ministries are not delivering, which partner is not trusted, which of our own
people are considered unreliable, and what the leadership privately expects
from the next reshuffle.

Nothing said is scandalous. All of it is accurate, which is worse, because
every rival now has a sourced account of the party's internal balance and
every partner has heard themselves discussed.

[? if left_in_government = 1: Our coalition partners heard their ministers
assessed by name.?][? if left_in_government = 0: Our opposition allies heard
exactly how little we think of the joint arrangement.?]
[? if razem_in_left = 1: Razem's deputies were the sharpest voices in the room
and are now the most quoted.?][? if razem_in_left = 0: Razem, outside the
common caucus, is enjoying this considerably.?]

- @broadcast_own_it
- @broadcast_discipline
- @broadcast_deflect

@broadcast_own_it
title: Publish the full recording and stand behind the argument
subtitle: Turns an accident into a claim of internal democracy; the partners we criticised do not forgive on that basis.
on-arrival: public_trust += 3; party_unity += 2; media_capacity += 2; ko_relation -= 5; psl_relation -= 4; p2050_relation -= 3; progressives_dissent -= 3; labor_dissent -= 2; left_poll_momentum += 0.1; news_headline = "Lewica publishes the leaked caucus in full and defends the argument"

We put the whole recording up ourselves, uncut, with a short note: this is
what a party arguing about its own record sounds like, and the alternative is
a party that does not argue. Younger members and organisers respond to it
better than to any communications strategy of the previous year.

The people we named do not respond well at all. Two coalition partners now
know precisely how our deputies rate their ministers, and they will price that
into the next negotiation rather than the next press conference.

- @poland_event_queue: Publish the recording

@broadcast_discipline
title: Run an internal inquiry and tighten caucus procedure
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Restores the leadership's control and confirms that the interesting arguments now happen elsewhere.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The party has no staff free to run an internal procedure review.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; party_unity += 5; barons_strength += 2; progressives_dissent += 4; razem_dissent += 3 if razem_in_left = 1; media_capacity += 1; public_trust -= 1; news_headline = "The caucus tightens its rules after the accidental broadcast"

The inquiry finds a technical failure and a procedural one, and the procedure
is rewritten: fewer people in the room, agendas circulated in advance,
positions agreed before the meeting rather than in it.

The meetings become shorter and quieter. The argument does not stop; it moves
to the corridors and to three journalists who now have better sourcing inside
our caucus than they did before we fixed the stream.

- @poland_event_queue: Adopt the new caucus rules

@broadcast_deflect
title: Say nothing and let the clips run
subtitle: The cheapest response; the interpretation is then written entirely by rivals.
on-arrival: party_unity -= 3; media_capacity -= 2; ko_relation -= 2; konf_poll_momentum += 0.15; public_trust -= 2; left_poll_momentum -= 0.15; news_headline = "The caucus clips are edited by everyone except the party they came from"

No statement is issued. The recording is edited by other people into a week of
material about a divided, resentful Left that despises its own partners.

By the time we would like to explain the context, the context has been decided
by accounts with more reach than ours. The internal argument continues, now
with the additional knowledge that anything said in it may be broadcast.

- @poland_event_queue: Let the clips run
